WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:01:11.360
(Casting)

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:14.200
"He just goes 'Top Die' and then he gets a solo kill."

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:21.640
Jung “Impact” Eon-yeong was born in March 1995 in South Korea and was

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:26.640
an early adopter to League of Legends, playing on the North American server before the Korean

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:27.760
one was released.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:32.440
But once it was released in December 2011, his talent attracted attention.

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:37.760
He first joined Xenics Storm as a support player in 2012 where he helped them qualify

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:40.450
for the upcoming OGN spring season.

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:46.390
Xenics made a deep run in that inaugural Champions tournament, losing an extremely close semifinal

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:47.760
to MiG Frost.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:57.080
(Casting)

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:02.000
They won the third place match against OP, finishing their first season in third,

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.680
and regrouped for summer.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:08.379
But after Storm’s run in the summer ended in the quarterfinals, Impact entered a period

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:09.920
of roster turbulence.

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:15.660
He left Xenics to play for Tempest, but that roster was eventually acquired by Xenics.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:21.520
But by February 2013, Impact left the org for good and found a new team.

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:28.080
Impact found a place on SK Telecom T12’s newly-formed roster.

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:33.200
And having switched to top lane near the end of his time with Xenics, he’d stay in that

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:34.640
role with SKT.

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:39.280
It was clear almost immediately that SKT had something special in that roster.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:41.500
(Casting)

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:49.220
Playing alongside bengi, Faker, Piglet and PoohManDu, SKT T12 placed third in the spring

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:52.170
but came into their own by the time summer started.

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:58.000
And, in a dazzling final outside the Jamsil Olympic Stadium in Seoul in August 2013, Impact

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:00.860
Impact won his first OGN title.

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:10.360
(Casting)

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:15.520
Impact played well in that series, showing that he was a top-level player in his position.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:20.080
But his performance was somewhat obscured by one of his teammates’ growing shadow.

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:45.720
(Casting)

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:50.680
With rivals KT Rolster Bullets missing out on the World Championship that year, Impact

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:57.540
and SK Telecom T1 K looked like the strongest Korean team headed to the year’s biggest tournament.

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:08.240
SKT entered the group stage ready to assert their dominance.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:12.500
But they were surprised by the quality of teams that they faced early on.

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:32.820
And when they dropped a group stage game to China’s OMG, SKT, the strongest team

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:36.700
heading into the tournament, realized that they needed to get serious.

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:38.760
That this was no vacation.

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:53.600
SKT exited the group stage 7-1 and, in the quarterfinals, they matched up against one

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:56.640
of four teams seeded directly into the bracket:

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:58.240
The Gamania Bears.

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:00.060
They dispatched them, 2-0.

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:16.000
(Casting)

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:20.080
So far, SKT had only dropped one game throughout the tournament.

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:23.660
But they faced a serious obstacle in semifinals.

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:26.780
Najin Sword.

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:32.960
The winners of Champions Winter 2012-2013, Najin qualified directly for the quarterfinals

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:39.340
at Worlds, so by the time SKT sat down to face them, they had little recent information on them.

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:54.000
And perhaps because of that unfamiliarity, it was a close series from the get-go,

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:59.620
"That leaves us at 2-2 now. We're gonna go into the last game, it's anyones."

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:05.240
SKT got off to an early lead thanks to superior laning and an edge in objectives,

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:09.240
but Najin continued to apply pressure with their pick-focused comp.

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:14.420
In the end, SKT’s superior teamfighting was too much for Najin, and they ended the

00:06:14.420 --> 00:06:17.760
game quickly off the back of one clean fight.

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:43.160
(Casting)

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:48.680
It was the first time since the Korean summer final that SKT had really been tested.

00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:54.320
For a roster with young players, many in their first year of competitive play, they were

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:56.360
able to stay calm and composed.

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:59.560
And they dismantled Najin in the clutch.

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:02.160
"When they win they win so quickly."

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:07.560
"That was one team fight. Into game over for SKT."

00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:13.020
There was one series left between Impact, SKT and history.

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:14.480
One stage.

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:18.100
And it would be one of esports’ biggest
to date.

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:22.340
Though Season 2’s World Championship had been one of the biggest esports events held

00:07:22.340 --> 00:07:27.520
in the West ever at the time, Season 3’s offering was on a completely different scale.

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:32.720
And it was in a packed Staples Centre, to the roar of thousands of fans, that Impact

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:36.560
and his team played for an engraving on the Summoner’s Cup.

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:41.480
Though Impact was no stranger to playing in front of large crowds, this would be his biggest

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:42.500
so far.

00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:47.689
And in the lead-up to the finals, SKT’s players were candid about some nerves related

00:07:47.689 --> 00:07:48.860
to the big series.

00:07:57.320 --> 00:08:03.440
Their opponents in the final were Royal Club, a Chinese team that bulldozed OMG and Fnatic

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:05.059
on their way to the finals.

00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:13.520
The team’s big personalities and Tabe’s English interviews quickly made them a fan favorite.

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:19.440
But it wasn’t just popular perception, Royal’s clean dismantling of OMG and Fnatic

00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:24.600
the two other teams to go 7-1 in groups convinced many analysts of their strength.

00:08:24.780 --> 00:08:25.980
"I'm going with Royal"

00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:28.480
"I'm going to have to go ahead and choose Royal as well."

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:29.160
"Royal all the way."

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:34.660
(Casting)

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:39.500
After going up 2-0 against Royal, SKT looked set to sweep the series.

00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:44.420
As far as Impact was concerned, once they locked in Jax, one of his signature picks,

00:08:44.420 --> 00:08:45.920
the tournament was his.

00:08:49.400 --> 00:09:15.120
(Casting)

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:18.840
In the end Worlds 2013 belonged to Korea.

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:21.540
It belonged to SKT.

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:23.500
It belonged to Impact.

00:09:24.040 --> 00:09:29.020
For Impact, and the rest of SKT, standing on that stage and hoisting that trophy

00:09:29.020 --> 00:09:31.840
was the culmination of their careers so far.

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:33.420
But they wanted more.

00:09:33.980 --> 00:09:35.160
And they got it.

00:09:36.660 --> 00:09:42.060
SKT went undefeated in the group stage of Champions winter 2013-2014.

00:09:42.460 --> 00:09:45.640
They swept Samsung Blue in the quarterfinals.

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:51.460
They swept the KT Rolster Bullets in the semifinals.

00:09:53.980 --> 00:09:57.380
Then, they swept Samsung Ozone in the finals.

00:10:00.840 --> 00:10:03.820
15-0, in six series.

00:10:03.820 --> 00:10:07.900
It’s still the only perfect season in the history of Korean League.

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:10.700
SKT were on top of the world.

00:10:11.140 --> 00:10:15.680
But it was the last time Impact would taste
success in Korea.

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:21.460
2014 was a slump year for SKT.

00:10:21.640 --> 00:10:27.620
Although 2013 had been all about the mechanical prowess of SKT’s stars, two new rosters

00:10:27.620 --> 00:10:33.420
had begun to rise to prominence, ones with the mechanics and teamplay to surpass SKT.

00:10:33.460 --> 00:10:39.280
Samsung Blue and Samsung Ozone (later Samsung White) were two of the strongest rosters in

00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:41.140
the world in 2014.

00:10:41.140 --> 00:10:46.980
And the latter knocked SKT out of Champions Spring and Summer in the quarterfinals both times.

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:54.120
Then, SKT missed out on Worlds after losing to a surging Najin White Shield in the Regional Finals.

00:10:54.280 --> 00:10:58.540
SKT didn’t even get a chance to defend their
World Championship.

00:10:58.820 --> 00:11:03.080
Fans of the team had begun to see some of the players as dead weight.

00:11:03.140 --> 00:11:06.940
Faker was still a god, but his teammates couldn’t keep up.

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:11.800
And though Impact played well in in the series against White Shield, there was a clear sense

00:11:11.820 --> 00:11:14.120
that changes needed to be made.

00:11:14.200 --> 00:11:18.660
At the same time, Riot Korea announced that they would be removing sisters teams for the

00:11:18.660 --> 00:11:20.110
2015 season.

00:11:20.110 --> 00:11:26.340
And, as some teams consolidated their rosters there was also a huge surge of players leaving

00:11:26.340 --> 00:11:31.760
Korea, as stars from League’s top region sought glory and money abroad.

00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:34.460
Impact was one of them.

00:11:34.470 --> 00:11:39.430
According to reports at the time, Impact chose to leave the roster, and he’s said that

00:11:39.430 --> 00:11:41.240
he was ready for something new.

00:11:56.160 --> 00:12:01.120
He joined Team Impulse, and got off to a decent start with his new team, finishing the regular

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:04.520
season in fourth place with an 11-7 record.

00:12:04.620 --> 00:12:10.500
They lost to Team SoloMid in the playoff semifinals, then to Team Liquid in the third-place match

00:12:10.500 --> 00:12:12.160
to finish the Spring in fourth.

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:20.260
Impulse played high-kill, entertaining games, often with off-meta picks like Yasuo, Fizz and Lee Sin.

00:12:20.440 --> 00:12:26.420
But even as he embraced and enabled his team’s chaotic playstyle, Impact was a stable force

00:12:26.420 --> 00:12:27.360
in the top lane.

00:12:27.360 --> 00:12:38.560
(Casting)

00:12:38.560 --> 00:12:42.980
For the first time in his career, he was not overshadowed by mechanical prodigies.

00:12:42.980 --> 00:12:48.680
Instead, he was the veteran presence among a group of relatively inexperienced players.

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:53.780
Though he was still working on English, Impact had quietly become more than just one of the

00:12:53.820 --> 00:12:55.940
mechanically strongest tops in NA.

00:12:56.000 --> 00:13:00.800
By the end of summer he was also learning to put his over-confident past behind him

00:13:00.800 --> 00:13:02.160
and become a leader.

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:06.720
"At first it's kind of intimidating. But I do have a lot of respect for him. He has a lot of knowledge.

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:12.820
He's really patient with the game. So it's been quite a big learning experience for me."

00:13:19.160 --> 00:13:24.030
And heading into playoffs in Summer 2015, TiP
were a legitimate contender.

00:13:24.030 --> 00:13:27.980
They finished the regular season in third place at 12-6.

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:33.540
Their wild and unpredictable style had allowed them to take wins off of teams trying for

00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:39.700
a more standard style, and their powerful solo lanes in Impact and XiaoWeiXiao were a force.

00:13:39.700 --> 00:13:48.920
(Casting)

00:13:48.920 --> 00:13:54.360
But, after XiaoWeiXiao was banned due to Elo boosting, Impulse’s unpredictable style

00:13:54.360 --> 00:13:55.720
wasn’t enough.

00:13:56.000 --> 00:14:01.360
They lost to CLG in the semifinals and Team Liquid in the third-place match.

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:04.360
Impulse finished fourth again.

00:14:04.360 --> 00:14:08.900
It was enough to earn Impact and company a
spot in the Regional Gauntlet.

00:14:09.320 --> 00:14:13.480
For Impact, it was a chance for him to make it back to Worlds.

00:14:13.480 --> 00:14:16.180
To stand on that stage one more time.

00:14:16.500 --> 00:14:18.100
And it started off well.

00:14:18.200 --> 00:14:24.120
But after a reverse sweep at the hands of Cloud9 — who were on their way to a miraculous

00:14:24.120 --> 00:14:29.180
run to Worlds — Impulse finished short of a spot on the world stage.

00:14:29.560 --> 00:14:34.260
For Impact, it was the second time in a row he’d been eliminated in the Gauntlet,

00:14:34.260 --> 00:14:36.860
falling just shy of a Worlds berth.

00:14:38.200 --> 00:14:42.480
Impact joined a new team, NRG Esports in November.

00:14:42.480 --> 00:14:48.420
But despite a relatively strong start to the spilt, NRG failed to make much of a splash,

00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:51.120
and were eliminated in the quarterfinals.

00:14:51.580 --> 00:14:57.720
Though Impact had earned the respect of North American fans, he had yet to really prove himself.

00:14:58.020 --> 00:15:02.860
He might have been seen as one of NA’s strongest top laners, but he had yet to play for one

00:15:02.860 --> 00:15:04.600
of NA’s strongest teams.

00:15:04.700 --> 00:15:07.140
It was time for another change.

00:15:07.520 --> 00:15:13.800
It was on Cloud9 that Impact cemented himself as one of the strongest top laners in the region.

00:15:13.800 --> 00:15:17.880
And Impact’s dominant laning helped to coin a new phrase.

00:15:17.880 --> 00:15:21.920
“He just goes 'Top Die' and then he gets a solo kill, so I don't know he's really good.”

00:15:21.920 --> 00:15:26.340
And, throughout the end of the split, Impact was making waves again

00:15:26.340 --> 00:15:28.840
for stellar individual plays with the team,

00:15:28.840 --> 00:15:33.560
prompting analysts and players to herald him as the region’s best top laner.

00:15:33.560 --> 00:15:39.840
This time, Impact wasn’t eliminated early in playoffs and Cloud9 made it to the finals.

00:15:39.920 --> 00:15:41.840
The series was close

00:15:41.840 --> 00:15:59.760
(Casting)

00:15:59.840 --> 00:16:03.580
but in the end, TSM was able to clutch the series out.

00:16:03.580 --> 00:16:23.380
(Casting)

00:16:24.560 --> 00:16:29.820
This time, after defeating Team EnVyUs and Immortals in the Regional Gauntlet, Impact

00:16:29.820 --> 00:16:35.300
was able to secure a spot at the World Championship, his first time back at the game’s biggest

00:16:35.300 --> 00:16:38.100
stage since he won it in 2013.

00:16:48.540 --> 00:16:56.740
Cloud9 landed in a rough group at Worlds 2016, facing off against SK Telecom T1, I May and

00:16:56.740 --> 00:16:58.220
Flash Wolves.

00:16:58.400 --> 00:17:04.140
With SKT a clear favorite to make it out of the group, Cloud9 were expected to struggle

00:17:04.160 --> 00:17:05.720
to find the second seed.

00:17:06.080 --> 00:17:10.180
This was especially true against the Taiwanese players on Flash Wolves,

00:17:10.180 --> 00:17:14.400
who had built up a formidable reputation at international events.

00:17:14.400 --> 00:17:19.300
But the group stage draw meant something else for Impact: he would face off against his

00:17:19.300 --> 00:17:21.560
old teammates, his old coach.

00:17:21.760 --> 00:17:25.060
He would play against the organization that made his career.

00:17:37.480 --> 00:18:01.520
(Casting)

00:18:01.560 --> 00:18:06.040
In the end, SKT beat Cloud9 in both their group stage games,

00:18:08.160 --> 00:18:13.040
But, thanks to some struggles from Flash Wolves and consistent play on Cloud9’s part,

00:18:13.140 --> 00:18:25.200
(Casting)

00:18:25.200 --> 00:18:30.500
The NA team was able to secure the second seed
to quarterfinals with a 3-3 record.

00:18:30.500 --> 00:18:35.200
In quarterfinals, Cloud9 faced off against eventual finalists Samsung Galaxy

00:18:35.280 --> 00:18:41.620
(Casting)

00:18:41.740 --> 00:18:44.100
and were dismantled 3-0.

00:18:44.320 --> 00:18:46.860
Cloud9 were not expected to win.

00:18:47.120 --> 00:18:50.640
They went further than any other NA team at the tournament.

00:18:50.840 --> 00:18:55.000
But against one of the best teams in the world, they couldn’t hold up.

00:18:55.000 --> 00:19:00.600
"Look at back at how they got here and Impact was playing so good in the Top lane and teams made sure

00:19:00.600 --> 00:19:02.940
to shut that down as much as they could."

00:19:02.940 --> 00:19:07.080
"And it definitely seemed like that's what Cloud9 was lacking and the fact that Impact couldn't get going

00:19:07.080 --> 00:19:11.040
again, not saying it's his fault, teams made sure that Impact couldn't get going again."

00:19:11.040 --> 00:19:16.440
It might have been a decent performance for NA on the international stage, but for Impact

00:19:16.440 --> 00:19:22.299
it was another year without an NA LCS title, and another year without any notable results

00:19:22.300 --> 00:19:24.220
on the international stage.

00:19:25.120 --> 00:19:28.880
In Spring 2017, Cloud9 came close again.

00:19:29.060 --> 00:19:33.900
They placed second in the regular season, with Impact taking 11 “player of the game

00:19:33.900 --> 00:19:38.580
awards,” tied for second with Hauntzer, just behind Dardoch’s 12.

00:19:38.700 --> 00:19:44.420
But after making their way to another finals against TSM, they fell in a close 3-2 series

00:19:44.420 --> 00:19:45.800
to miss out on MSI.

00:19:45.840 --> 00:19:49.120
And in the summer split, Cloud9 faltered.

00:19:49.120 --> 00:19:55.060
"Heartbreak for Cloud9 this is a game that Cloud9 had a 10 thousand gold lead and three dead inhibitors.

00:19:55.060 --> 00:19:58.960
And they full built Tristana, and they failed to attack Nexus turrets.

00:19:58.960 --> 00:20:02.920
To play it right to close the game, their tanks got caught out shoved into the fight."

00:20:03.060 --> 00:20:08.820
Yet again, TSM hoisted the trophy while Cloud9 were forced to play in the Regional Finals

00:20:08.820 --> 00:20:10.240
for a shot at Worlds.

00:20:10.240 --> 00:20:19.200
(Casting)

00:20:19.200 --> 00:20:24.020
This time, Cloud9 had to make it through the Play-In Stage, where they swept their group

00:20:24.020 --> 00:20:26.480
and qualifier match without dropping 
a game.

00:20:26.480 --> 00:20:39.120
(Casting)

00:20:39.120 --> 00:20:45.400
But once they qualified for the main event, Impact and the rest of Cloud9 faced stiff competition.

00:20:45.660 --> 00:20:51.900
With shades of 2016, they once again had to face SKT, along with tough international teams

00:20:51.900 --> 00:20:55.400
in EDward Gaming and ahq e-Sports Club.

00:20:55.680 --> 00:21:00.580
Once again, Cloud9 made it out with a 3-3 record as the second seed.

00:21:00.800 --> 00:21:04.940
Once again they were the only NA team to advance to the Top 8.

00:21:05.040 --> 00:21:09.840
This time, though, Cloud9 wouldn’t be facing off against a top Korean team.

00:21:10.220 --> 00:21:15.900
They were seeded against World Elite, a Chinese roster that had showed strong results in groups,

00:21:15.920 --> 00:21:17.560
but who looked mortal.

00:21:17.920 --> 00:21:20.040
Cloud9 had a fighting chance.

00:21:20.040 --> 00:21:21.380
And they knew it.

00:21:21.760 --> 00:21:26.860
The series went back and forth, with everything coming down to a pivotal Game 5.

00:21:26.860 --> 00:21:42.660
(Casting)

00:21:42.660 --> 00:21:48.640
But WE was able to push a mid game lead to a huge gold advantage, and in the end Cloud9

00:21:48.680 --> 00:21:50.340
couldn’t clutch the series.

00:21:50.340 --> 00:22:00.240
(Casting)

00:22:00.240 --> 00:22:05.600
For Impact, it was the end of his third year in NA, and after two quarterfinal exits at

00:22:05.620 --> 00:22:11.920
Worlds and too many almosts in the NA LCS, it was clear that something had to change.

00:22:12.900 --> 00:22:19.240
The end of 2017 signaled a new, franchised era in North America and with big money flowing

00:22:19.240 --> 00:22:23.060
into the scene, there was a huge turnover in rosters.

00:22:23.220 --> 00:22:28.600
Orgs vied for top talent to secure the best possible franchised team for themselves,

00:22:28.600 --> 00:22:33.060
and after three splits with Cloud9, Impact moved to a new roster.

00:22:33.760 --> 00:22:38.200
Team Liquid attracted a lot of attention heading into the Spring Split.

00:22:38.340 --> 00:22:44.500
Impact joined TL alongside former-CLG core Xmithie, Pobelter and Doublelift

00:22:44.500 --> 00:22:46.820
and Korean support Olleh.

00:22:47.160 --> 00:22:51.880
It was a roster that boasted proven synergy and very strong lanes.

00:22:51.880 --> 00:22:54.340
People expected Team Liquid to dominate.

00:22:54.340 --> 00:22:56.940
"Yeah this roster was basically built to win, that's why I joined it."

00:22:57.000 --> 00:22:59.200
The regular season saw some stumbles,

00:22:59.280 --> 00:23:02.580
"And you can see Impact. Devastated."

00:23:02.620 --> 00:23:07.340
and despite a strong start, by the end of the split Team Liquid were caught in a convoluted

00:23:07.340 --> 00:23:09.880
tie near the top of the standings.

00:23:09.880 --> 00:23:14.120
They entered playoffs in quarterfinals, with a lot of questions about their play.

00:23:14.320 --> 00:23:17.520
But those questions were dismissed almost immediately.

00:23:17.520 --> 00:23:34.960
(Casting)

00:23:34.960 --> 00:23:39.260
All season long it was clear that Team Liquid had the talent to win.

00:23:39.260 --> 00:24:02.240
(Casting)

00:24:02.240 --> 00:24:05.680
They’d assembled a roster stacked with individual
talent.

00:24:05.720 --> 00:24:09.640
But until playoffs, they’d been unable to capitalize on their potential.

00:24:09.980 --> 00:24:12.200
And they peaked at the right time.

00:24:12.280 --> 00:24:22.540
(Casting)

00:24:22.540 --> 00:24:28.120
In the end only one team stood between Liquid and the NA LCS trophy.

00:24:28.120 --> 00:24:30.080
100 Thieves.

00:24:30.080 --> 00:24:32.460
Liquid were able to ride the momentum.

00:24:32.520 --> 00:24:42.020
(Casting)

00:24:42.020 --> 00:24:45.880
And the rest of the series was about one team. And one team only.

00:24:45.880 --> 00:25:22.540
(Casting)

00:25:22.620 --> 00:25:29.000
Liquid dominated the rest of finals, and as the confetti rained down on them, Impact was elated.

00:25:29.740 --> 00:25:34.980
After more than three years in North America, and so many close-but-not-quites,

00:25:34.980 --> 00:25:40.560
the win was Impact’s first title since Champions
Winter 2013-14.

00:25:43.680 --> 00:25:49.060
In the four and a half years since then, he’d moved across the world, developed his skills,

00:25:49.060 --> 00:25:55.520
become a leader, learned a new language and made many, many top laners die.

00:25:55.660 --> 00:26:01.480
His peers in the NA LCS had come to revere him as one of the best tops to ever play in the region.

00:26:01.780 --> 00:26:07.720
there’s no question that Impact has already cemented his place in League’s history books.

00:26:08.140 --> 00:26:12.340
Impact has evolved from a brash, young player to a mature,

00:26:12.360 --> 00:26:16.280
veteran presence who’s capable of fitting into any team.

00:26:16.340 --> 00:26:19.640
But he’d never been able to hoist that trophy.

00:26:19.860 --> 00:26:27.360
Never conquered his region. But on that hot day in Florida, Impact was back on top.

00:26:29.080 --> 00:26:34.220
Thanks for watching. If you want more great content just like this, be sure to hit the subscribe button.

